**Over `Q`, the `p=0` slice is a reduced curve of degree five**, in two explicit families:

- Family A: `x1=⋯=x5=0`, `2x0^2=9`, `q` free  
- Family B: `x1=x2=x4=x5=0`, `4x3^3+81=0`, `x0=x3 q`, `q` free  

On both, `p=r8=0`, so `r8'=0`, which kills actual trajectories on those components via `r8'=j/(9u)`.

**That does not decide `I ∩ Q[p]`.**** The elimination ideal is nonzero if and only if there is no dimension-one component on which `p` varies. A characteristic-zero Groebner basis of the unsaturated ideal was not obtained.

Modulo many primes the complementary picture is stable: a zero-dimensional `p≠0` scheme of degree `1188` whose `p`-minpoly is a **degree-70 polynomial in `p^9`**. Four-prime Farey reconstruction of that polynomial failed (heights too large). msolve’s characteristic-zero “empty” report on the saturation disagrees with Singular at the same prime and was discarded.

